WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.519
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with attorney Mark Bankston, who's a partner

00:00:03.519 --> 00:00:09.520
at Fera and Ball and is lead counsel in several
defamation lawsuits against Alex Jones and

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:10.640
Infowars.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.690
Some people may recognize him from dozens
and dozens and dozens of viral YouTube videos

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.500
that we all looked at extensively.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.039
Mark, great having you on today.

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:21.880
Speaker 4: Hey, thanks for having me, David.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:28.480
Speaker 1: So how how did you end up doing
these cases and representing a variety of

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.270
different plaintiffs here in defamation against
Alex Jones and Infowars?

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:36.239
Speaker 4: I'm glad you asked me that, because,
you know, a lot of people just start and end

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:37.760
with these Sandy Hook cases.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.300
But the first lawsuit I actually brought against
Jones was for a young man in the Boston area

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:48.530
who was inexplicably accused of being the
Parkland shooter in the 2018 shootings in

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:49.610
Florida.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:55.429
And at that time, I was in touch with this
young man, and he had never even been to Florida.

00:00:55.429 --> 00:00:59.660
It turned out that he was accused on four
channels of being the Parkland shooter, and

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:01.569
it was because he was wearing a communist
themed T-shirt.

00:01:01.569 --> 00:01:04.850
He had the the comedy t shirt.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:08.829
That's the communist leaders with lampshades
on their heads drinking from solo cups.

00:01:08.829 --> 00:01:13.409
So a visual pun on Communist Party or Infowars
just ran with that because they were so excited

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:17.460
that they could claim that the shooter was
a communist and they took this kid's picture

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.939
who was being teased on Fortuyn and put it
all over the world.

00:01:20.939 --> 00:01:24.700
He reached out to me and we brought suit back
in March of 2018.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:27.920
And when we did that, and that made national
headlines.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.090
Several Sandy Hook parents picked up the phone
and they said, you know, we've been trying

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.470
to get somebody to bring these suits for us,
somebody who will take on Alex Jones.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.431
People were saying it's too hard or it's too
big of a fight.

00:01:37.431 --> 00:01:39.140
We need to find a lawyer who would do it.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.229
And I said, you found one.

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:47.210
Speaker 1: So in in general, with these types
of cases, what is it you have to prove to

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:50.929
actually be able to prevail in a case?

00:01:50.929 --> 00:01:57.030
Because it's not just that a statement was
pejorative, but there are specific requirements

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.340
that have to be met.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:00.150
And do they vary state by state?

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:01.920
Speaker 4: They do a little bit.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:02.920
Right.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.079
Our common law of defamation is as old as
the Magna Carta, but every state has a little

00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:08.470
bit different rules.

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.680
But it basically boils down to this you have
to prove three things.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.490
You have to prove that they published a false
statement and that the second thing you have

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:21.000
to prove is that it was of or concerning a
specific individual.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.570
And the third thing you have to prove is that
that false statement caused them some sort

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.050
of damages.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:30.130
And it's interesting because, you know, these
cases actually ended up having default judgments

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:33.860
on the liability, but it was never really
something we needed.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:37.380
It was sort of, you know, in a lot of cases,
that would be an enormous victory.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:42.190
But in this case, it was never going to be
difficult to prove that Alex Jones published

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.630
false statements about these people that caused
them damages.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.340
This case was always about how much Alex Jones
needed to be punished.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:54.190
Speaker 1: When it comes to the judgment that
you obtained, which made a number of headlines

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:57.000
that close to $50 million.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.830
There are state limits on what can actually
be collected.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:02.830
Is that correct?

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:07.620
I mean, what what is it looking like will
actually be the liability for Jones of the

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.620
$49 million?

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.100
Speaker 4: It's interesting because everyone's
talking about this right now, and most of

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:15.850
the people who are talking about it are not
familiar with Texas law.

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:19.910
There is a punitive damage cap statute that
was passed by the legislature.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:23.810
However, it does not apply under all factual
situations.

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:26.450
There are large amounts of exceptions to it.

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:27.450
Okay.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.860
We are going to be moving toward the injury
of judgment sometime later this week and will

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.610
have a hearing with the court soon.

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:36.750
And we're very confident that those caps aren't
going to apply.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.120
There are several reasons why under this case
and those will be discussed at the hearing

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:41.120
coming up.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.250
But we believe it will be the full $50 million
verdict.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:48.870
The defendants are going to try to argue a
cap applies, which basically limits us to

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:53.720
$750,000 in additional damages per claim per
defendant.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.720
There are three claims with two defendants
each.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.490
That would be a practical cap of 4.5 million
at the end of the day, with costs in the interest,

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.120
it would be a total verdict of about ten.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:08.050
But we actually do believe that we're going
to have going to be able to get around those

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:12.010
caps, with the exceptions, and that will be
collecting the entire $50 million verdict.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:16.290
Speaker 1: What's your best assessment of
the real financial situation at this point

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.040
of Alex Jones and Infowars?

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:24.780
Because as many of you in my audience know,
there has been an assertion of bankruptcy.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.960
And as the judge in your case astutely pointed
out, just because you claim bankruptcy doesn't

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:31.830
mean you actually are bankrupt.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:36.440
There are some statements I've seen from Jones
basically copping to the reality that it's

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:41.100
a strategic move specifically to try to limit
the amount that can actually be collected

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.810
from him in this and maybe in other cases.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.980
What's your best assessment of his financial
position?

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:48.320
Speaker 4: It's a real difficult question
to answer.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:52.870
You know, we had some very limited and contradictory
evidence on that at trial.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.040
And we know that his revenues are enormous.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.570
But what's harder to understand is what's
going on behind the scenes.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:04:59.570
Right.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:04.090
We have now started to encounter some very
disturbing evidence that is showing Mr. Jones

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:07.670
has been pulling millions and millions out
of his business after these default judgments.

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:12.410
In fact, in just just this last year, Jones
used his business like a cash machine to the

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.910
tune of $60 million that he removed from the
company.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:20.840
We really don't know what is all there, but
we know there is a very significant amount

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:22.310
of money.

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:26.000
We feel, though, that by the end of all of
these lawsuits, you know, there's there's

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.960
eight more plaintiffs up in Connecticut.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.450
I have two more I'm taking to trial on a Sandy
Hook case.

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:32.420
Plus another issue that partially.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:36.310
In case we're pretty sure these verdicts are
going to wipe them out.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.830
But actually chasing him down for that money
and the collections process may become as

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:42.210
interesting as the lawsuits themselves.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:47.180
Speaker 1: In a case like this, if someone
just sort of like doesn't wire the money,

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.110
for lack of a better term.

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:53.290
What mechanisms are there like, you know,
in evictions, eventually the men with guns

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:55.450
show up and kick you out sort of thing.

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:57.410
Like what ultimately happens in a case like
this?

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:01.389
Speaker 4: Well, if it was a normal case and
we were collecting outside of bankruptcy,

00:06:01.389 --> 00:06:02.930
we have lots of tools at our disposal.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:07.400
We can do a writ of execution that essentially
sends the sheriff over to Infowars and start

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.660
seizing computers and cameras.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:12.300
Those are the tools that you would normally
have at your disposal.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.169
But this bankruptcy proceeding is going to
be very interesting because now you're going

00:06:16.169 --> 00:06:21.400
to have a federal bankruptcy judge and a U.S.
trustee who are overseeing the financial scrutiny

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.860
of Infowars.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:27.290
That process itself is going to be very, very
dangerous for Mr. Jones.

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:31.270
And we I think by the end of all of that,
we're not going to need to resort to sending

00:06:31.270 --> 00:06:32.270
sheriffs out.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:36.290
We think the federal enforcement processes
and the bankruptcy court are going to divide

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:37.400
up the Jones's empire.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:42.560
Speaker 1: When you say pulling money out
of businesses, is that and kind of running

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.380
it through various LLC?

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:45.870
Is is it all domestic?

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:48.730
Do you have evidence that money is being offshored
now?

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:50.960
Speaker 4: You're thinking way too high level.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.699
You're thinking that they're sophisticated
financial operators.

00:06:53.699 --> 00:06:57.910
Yeah, I'm literally talking about just money
straight, drawn out of the company like it's

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:03.530
an ATM and put into Jones's hands cash, 60
million cash taken out of the company, not

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:05.310
routed through another LLC.

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:06.480
Not anything like this, but.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.800
But denoted on the balance sheets purely as
a member draw.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:14.580
And Jones is the only member of free speech
systems, so he really is just using the company

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:15.870
like a piggy bank.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:19.190
Where exactly that money went and what happened
to it is some questions that are going to

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:20.400
be answered in the coming months.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.370
Speaker 1: And then again, you're talking
about physical cash.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.410
Speaker 4: I don't you know, that's harder
to say.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:31.910
That really is I sort of don't suspect that
there was giant briefcases full of 60 million.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:32.910
Right.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:36.760
Like, I have to think that there was some
sort of electronic transfer into certain accounts.

00:07:36.760 --> 00:07:39.389
What we don't know yet is what accounts those
went to.

00:07:39.389 --> 00:07:43.330
We know since that it was member draws that
that money was paid out to Alex Jones.

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:45.850
Where it went from there is very difficult
to know yet.

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:51.360
Speaker 1: So there were all sorts of just
things that to me were unprecedented I had

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:52.360
never seen before.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:58.500
Like for example, that there was a running
commentary on Infowars about the case as it

00:07:58.500 --> 00:07:59.790
was going on.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:05.270
And then there were exhibits brought in from
during the trial which were about the judge,

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.470
about the jury, etc..

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:09.130
How did you even manage this?

00:08:09.130 --> 00:08:14.360
Was it just like even during the trial you
needed a team watching every minute that was

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:15.540
produced and broadcast?

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:17.150
Speaker 4: Absolutely.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:20.660
And I had one luckily, one stuffed up is,
you know, we're in court all day.

00:08:20.660 --> 00:08:21.790
We can't do that.

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:22.790
Right.

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:26.310
You may be familiar with Dan Friesen of the
podcast Knowledge Fight, who I would consider

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:29.130
maybe the world's foremost authority on Alex
Jones.

00:08:29.130 --> 00:08:34.039
He had actually joined on as a consulting
expert pro-bono very early in the case and

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:37.919
in the knowledge fight team during the days
of trial would keep track of what was said,

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:40.809
clip out the parts that we were interested
in and provide them to us.

00:08:40.809 --> 00:08:43.079
Those became exhibits in the courtroom.

00:08:43.079 --> 00:08:47.420
And it's really funny when you think about
it, that those those things almost get lost

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:48.420
in the details.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:51.860
There's so many people talking about, for
instance, Jones and Bush on the stand about

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.180
his text messages.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:57.529
And it almost gets forgotten that there were
videos of them accusing the judge of pedophilia

00:08:57.529 --> 00:09:01.870
and saying that the trial was rigged and that
the jurors are a bunch of blue collar locals

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:03.490
who don't understand what's going on.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:06.629
If that had happened in any other trial, that
would be the top story.

00:09:06.629 --> 00:09:07.860
That would be the headline.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:10.940
But there's so much that went on in this trial
that almost gets lost in the details.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:17.059
Speaker 1: So the text message thing for people
who may not have seen or don't remember, there

00:09:17.059 --> 00:09:23.459
was this incredible situation where for days
you were sort of setting up a number of different

00:09:23.459 --> 00:09:28.680
denials about Alex Jones having had text messages
specifically related to Sandy Hook on his

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:29.680
phone.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.490
Then it turned out you actually had a digital
copy of what was on his phone because his

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:38.920
attorney accidentally, as far as we know,
sent that digital copy to you.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:43.619
And this became a huge flash point in the
trial and corporate media covered it, etc..

00:09:43.619 --> 00:09:48.639
First question, at any point, did you think
that maybe it was a deliberate accident in

00:09:48.639 --> 00:09:54.339
order to either try to argue for a mistrial
or in some way manipulate the situation in

00:09:54.339 --> 00:09:55.339
their favor?

00:09:55.339 --> 00:09:59.980
Speaker 4: I knew that it wasn't deliberate
in the sense that it was a strategy in Mr.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:01.079
Jones's favor.

00:10:01.079 --> 00:10:02.749
I knew that.

00:10:02.749 --> 00:10:05.139
I will admit that very briefly.

00:10:05.139 --> 00:10:09.259
I had the same sort of wild ideas in my head
that Roger Stone has been spreading the last

00:10:09.259 --> 00:10:10.310
few days of.

00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:14.579
Is, is and you know, we're no an agent of
the deep state for, you know, used to work

00:10:14.579 --> 00:10:16.499
at the DOJ for Eric Holder.

00:10:16.499 --> 00:10:19.610
Is he screwing over Alex Jones somewhere in
a couple of seconds.

00:10:19.610 --> 00:10:20.610
So you realize.

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.929
No, that's that's wild, wild thinking.

00:10:22.929 --> 00:10:27.529
The reality of it is, is you had an attorney
who was not used to doing civil law, decided

00:10:27.529 --> 00:10:31.129
he was going to take some money to come in
and represent Alex Jones purely for the fame

00:10:31.129 --> 00:10:32.129
and attention.

00:10:32.129 --> 00:10:35.119
He was not able to protect his clients data
like he needed to.

00:10:35.119 --> 00:10:40.290
And those were not, in my eyes, intentional
moves or strategic moves.

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:44.220
They were actually very serious breaches of
his duties to his client.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:47.029
And that just sometimes happened when somebody
is in over their head like this.

00:10:47.029 --> 00:10:52.740
Speaker 1: There was a large, you know, an
hour or so at least that I remember during

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:57.529
which a number of requests were made by Alex
Jones, counsel of the judge, when it came

00:10:57.529 --> 00:10:59.439
to that digital copy.

00:10:59.439 --> 00:11:05.920
And I don't I didn't fully grasp the sort
of fundamentals of what it was that they were

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:11.300
asking for on the basis that those that that
record was accidentally sent to you.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:17.199
What was it that they were asking for and
why was it that it was not granted by the

00:11:17.199 --> 00:11:18.199
judge?

00:11:18.199 --> 00:11:20.670
Speaker 4: Well, it's funny that you say you
were having trouble figuring out what they

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:23.140
were asking for, because they were we okay.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:27.470
We they were asking for things that made no
sense in the context.

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:31.850
They wanted the judge to just do a blanket
sealing order over the entire documents, but

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:35.600
they had no basis to just suddenly steal 2.3
gigabytes of information.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:37.089
That's not how these rules work.

00:11:37.089 --> 00:11:38.089
Okay.

00:11:38.089 --> 00:11:42.720
They actually had ten days from the time of
disclosure to specifically identify any documents

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:45.889
that either had a legal privilege like they
were between attorney.

00:11:45.889 --> 00:11:49.429
They were husband and wife, something like
that or that were confidential because they

00:11:49.429 --> 00:11:51.670
were a trade secret or sensitive in some way.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:53.589
And they just didn't do it.

00:11:53.589 --> 00:11:57.339
And so when they came into the court, having
violated all the rules to do that, and then

00:11:57.339 --> 00:12:02.350
were essentially saying that that that my
side had somehow acted inappropriately, that

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:04.100
was quickly dismissed by the judge.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:08.999
She actually did give them and the opportunity
to to in a day designate anything is confidential,

00:12:08.999 --> 00:12:10.720
which means that I could still have it.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:13.059
I just couldn't share it outside the courtroom.

00:12:13.059 --> 00:12:15.129
They actually didn't do any of that.

00:12:15.129 --> 00:12:19.550
So the second day of those hearings, the last
day of trial, basically the judge declared

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:20.550
them free and clear.

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:21.739
They're in my hands now.

00:12:21.739 --> 00:12:25.200
Is there going to become some bad news for
Mr. Jones?

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:32.929
Speaker 1: Could Mr. Jones argue against the
ultimate judgment on the basis that his counsel

00:12:32.929 --> 00:12:34.870
inadequately represented him?

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:38.579
Speaker 4: Interestingly, no, you have that
option in criminal law.

00:12:38.579 --> 00:12:39.579
Okay.

00:12:39.579 --> 00:12:42.269
If you're criminal defense lawyer provides
you ineffective assistance, that is a basis

00:12:42.269 --> 00:12:43.310
for appeal.

00:12:43.310 --> 00:12:45.180
But in civil law, it is not.

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:49.139
And I think it's a good rule, because I think
it'd be rare that a criminal lawyer would

00:12:49.139 --> 00:12:52.800
intentionally throw a case because his client's
going to end up in jail.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.449
But in a civil case, it's more foreseeable.

00:12:55.449 --> 00:13:00.189
And so we don't let ineffective assistance
be the basis of their defense, particularly

00:13:00.189 --> 00:13:01.189
in criminal law.

00:13:01.189 --> 00:13:04.230
A lot of people who have appointed counsel,
they can't choose who their lawyer is.

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:05.649
Mr. Jones got to choose this lawyer.

00:13:05.649 --> 00:13:09.559
Speaker 1: You revealed on the Young Turks
last week when speaking to our friend and

00:13:09.559 --> 00:13:15.089
experienced that in that digital copy, there
was a nude photo of Alex Jones wife sent to

00:13:15.089 --> 00:13:16.240
Roger Stone.

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:22.850
And since then, Erica Wolf Jones came out
and said that it was non-consensual.

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:27.550
It's not clear to me whether she means that
the picture was taken non-consensual.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:33.819
He or she knew about the picture, but it was
not consensual to send it to Roger Stone.

00:13:33.819 --> 00:13:36.399
What on earth is going on when it comes to
this?

00:13:36.399 --> 00:13:41.619
This is almost like a side story, but raising
a ton of different questions about the relationship

00:13:41.619 --> 00:13:46.290
between Jones and Stone and just bizarre things
that you often just never even see become

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:47.290
part of a trial.

00:13:47.290 --> 00:13:50.220
Is there anything else on that that you've
since found out?

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:53.549
Speaker 4: I mean, the only thing that I can
say that I can help clarify the record on

00:13:53.549 --> 00:13:57.639
that is because Jones is actually what I found
was interesting is after I discussed that,

00:13:57.639 --> 00:14:02.529
Jones actually showed the picture on his show,
which I thought was strange.

00:14:02.529 --> 00:14:06.509
But what I can tell you is from the nature
of the.

00:14:06.509 --> 00:14:08.089
It was definitely consensually taken.

00:14:08.089 --> 00:14:09.089
Right.

00:14:09.089 --> 00:14:12.079
It wasn't it wasn't something that he was
creep shotting her or something like that.

00:14:12.079 --> 00:14:16.509
The non consent issue comes that it was sent
apropos of nothing do Roger Stone and what

00:14:16.509 --> 00:14:19.959
seems to be a bid for his attention that was
totally ignored by Stone.

00:14:19.959 --> 00:14:24.689
Just a random picture that was sent and some
other messages trying to get his attention

00:14:24.689 --> 00:14:27.860
as terms of what's going on in the future
with those kinds of issues.

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:31.509
Because I brought that to the fore, because
it does look like a crime.

00:14:31.509 --> 00:14:33.819
And that's not that's not an okay thing to
do.

00:14:33.819 --> 00:14:34.819
Speaker 1: Oh, is that right?

00:14:34.819 --> 00:14:39.139
So it would be that it's even if the picture
was taken consensually to disseminate it is

00:14:39.139 --> 00:14:40.139
potentially criminal.

00:14:40.139 --> 00:14:41.139
Speaker 4: Correct.

00:14:41.139 --> 00:14:43.579
And I mean, there's some decent caveats to
that in Texas.

00:14:43.579 --> 00:14:47.769
There has to be basically an intent to harm
or embarrass or something like that.

00:14:47.769 --> 00:14:52.629
But sending your wife's picture to Roger Stone
nonconsensual, he pretty much meets that that

00:14:52.629 --> 00:14:54.379
definition.

00:14:54.379 --> 00:14:59.399
And so, you know, I'm glad that Miss Wolf
Jones knows about that now.

00:14:59.399 --> 00:15:03.529
And there's a lot of other things in those
text messages that will likely come out in

00:15:03.529 --> 00:15:07.540
due course, which raise other troubling issues
in Mr. Jones's life.

00:15:07.540 --> 00:15:11.889
And so as that process goes forward, there
may very well be more revelations about what's

00:15:11.889 --> 00:15:12.889
going on there.

00:15:12.889 --> 00:15:17.269
Speaker 1: Do you think I might have a defamation
case against Alex Jones?

00:15:17.269 --> 00:15:22.799
He did call me a predator about six months
ago because I have a paid membership program

00:15:22.799 --> 00:15:27.929
on my show where people can pay and get access
to an extra 20 minute show a day.

00:15:27.929 --> 00:15:30.509
I can't really say that I was damaged by it.

00:15:30.509 --> 00:15:33.220
In fact, it was probably the best possible
thing that's ever happened.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:35.579
To be honest, I probably don't have a case
right now.

00:15:35.579 --> 00:15:37.829
Speaker 4: I would figure it out for a couple
of reasons.

00:15:37.829 --> 00:15:39.839
One is you're very much a public figure now.

00:15:39.839 --> 00:15:43.049
You know, you're a guy in the involved in
the middle of the discourse.

00:15:43.049 --> 00:15:46.549
And in this country, we let you be pretty
reckless, right up to the edge about public

00:15:46.549 --> 00:15:47.549
figures.

00:15:47.549 --> 00:15:48.549
Right.

00:15:48.549 --> 00:15:49.549
And then the Sandy Hook parents.

00:15:49.549 --> 00:15:50.549
Right.

00:15:50.549 --> 00:15:54.660
But it's also funny that I just find it ironic
that here you are talking about a promotion

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:57.220
program you're doing on your your show for
more content.

00:15:57.220 --> 00:15:58.220
Right.

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:02.809
And here's a guy who's hawking, you know,
super male vitality force and he's calling

00:16:02.809 --> 00:16:03.809
you a predator.

00:16:03.809 --> 00:16:05.220
That's that's really funny to me.

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:09.929
I think it's also funny you bring this up
because somebody was asking me today to explain,

00:16:09.929 --> 00:16:11.959
said, can you who's David Pakman?

00:16:11.959 --> 00:16:12.959
What's the show about?

00:16:12.959 --> 00:16:15.980
Obviously an older person who is not watching
their news on the Internet.

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:16.980
Sure.

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:22.009
And the best thing I could come up with was
imagine if Alex Jones wasn't bad.

00:16:22.009 --> 00:16:26.369
Imagine if somebody was doing news and commentary
on the Internet in an independent way that

00:16:26.369 --> 00:16:29.959
wasn't just a grift to try to sell you pills
that turn you red, right?

00:16:29.959 --> 00:16:31.899
Like what if you had that?

00:16:31.899 --> 00:16:35.920
And you know, that's one of the reasons I
went on TNT to one of the reasons I was really

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:36.989
happy to come here.

00:16:36.989 --> 00:16:37.989
Yeah.

00:16:37.989 --> 00:16:39.999
Is because the solution sure isn't getting
through the corporate media.

00:16:39.999 --> 00:16:43.809
It's through shows like this and by people
who are taking it seriously, being responsible.

00:16:43.809 --> 00:16:46.049
Speaker 1: Well, I appreciate that.

00:16:46.049 --> 00:16:47.589
Last thing I want to ask you about.

00:16:47.589 --> 00:16:51.470
The last time I was in the same room with
Alex Jones was years ago at a radio conference

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:55.059
called The New Media Seminar, which I don't
even know if it exists anymore.

00:16:55.059 --> 00:16:58.209
And at the time my impressions were he's playing
a character.

00:16:58.209 --> 00:17:02.499
My impressions were that he got off the stage
after giving a sort of animated but still

00:17:02.499 --> 00:17:03.929
kind of normal speech.

00:17:03.929 --> 00:17:07.250
And he said something like, you know, I could
have really freaked them out if I wanted to.

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:12.610
And it seemed that he sort of understood that
the on air persona was not at all his sort

00:17:12.610 --> 00:17:13.610
of real life.

00:17:13.610 --> 00:17:17.639
In the last two years, from everything I've
seen and read and interactions with Jones

00:17:17.639 --> 00:17:22.209
out in public that people have, it's increasingly
seeming like the Infowars.

00:17:22.209 --> 00:17:24.980
Alex Jones is the real guy.

00:17:24.980 --> 00:17:29.220
In your experience, having been in the room
with him for as long as you were, is it is

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:31.179
it a character or is that the real Alex Jones?

00:17:31.179 --> 00:17:35.440
Speaker 4: You know, that is a question I
have struggled with since the beginning and

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:37.720
at the start I thought I knew the answer.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:39.450
And I think that has changed over time.

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:41.460
For me, it's you.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:46.669
You see, at first I was positive and I still
remain very positive that most of the things

00:17:46.669 --> 00:17:50.880
he said, for instance, about Sandy Hook, he
never believed he never believed there were

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:52.660
pictures of the children still alive.

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:56.480
He never really believed the school was shut
down and Stockton was a fake school.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:58.340
I don't think he believed any of that.

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:03.899
And so there was a time that I really believed
that he was a sophisticated operator, completely

00:18:03.899 --> 00:18:05.820
detached from the things he was saying.

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:08.639
But as we went through this whole process,
I deposed him several times.

00:18:08.639 --> 00:18:13.809
As we went through trial, I also realized
there is no off switch for Jones and there

00:18:13.809 --> 00:18:21.240
is something about spending every day wrapped
in your own B.S. that it starts to actually

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:22.630
affect you.

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:24.360
And there is a mindset.

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:29.580
Jones I think you're right when you say that
he is conscious of how his words appear to

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:32.930
people, he and he is any and he wants to try
to control that.

00:18:32.930 --> 00:18:39.310
But here we just saw him in this trial, totally
unable to turn off the freak flag just for

00:18:39.310 --> 00:18:41.350
10 minutes in front of that jury.

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:43.990
And it really shows that there is there's
two sides of Jones.

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:47.510
There's a very, very conscious manipulator
side to Jones.

00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:50.820
And then there is a side where he is not really
in control of himself.

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:53.049
Those two parts of himself are at war.

00:18:53.049 --> 00:18:56.470
And right now, the part of him that's out
of control has been winning handily for the

00:18:56.470 --> 00:18:57.580
last couple of years.

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:00.990
Speaker 1: Yeah, out of control is quite,
quite an understatement.

00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:04.429
Mark Bankston, partner at Farah and Ball.

00:19:04.429 --> 00:19:08.610
And lead counsel in a number of defamation
lawsuits against Alex Jones and Infowars.

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:10.389
Mark, really a pleasure having you on today.

00:19:10.389 --> 00:19:12.299
Speaker 4: Hey, really pleasure being here.

00:19:12.299 --> 00:19:15.520
If you ever have legal questions and want
to talk about some more stuff, please have

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:15.610
them back.

